WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.185
Welcome to episode five.

2
00:00:03.185 --> 00:00:07.478
Today we take our time with
automations: you’ll run one yourself,

3
00:00:07.478 --> 00:00:10.847
watch it handle a real
task, debug one that failed,

4
00:00:10.847 --> 00:00:14.282
and approve an outgoing
email — with your own click.

5
00:00:14.282 --> 00:00:15.735
We’ll go step by step.

6
00:00:15.735 --> 00:00:19.170
This page — the run
journal — is where we’ll end up.

7
00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:21.350
But let’s start at the beginning.

8
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.284
This is a project board —
episode six tours it properly.

9
00:00:33.284 --> 00:00:38.208
For now, just look at the To do
column: new tasks, nobody assigned yet.

10
00:00:38.208 --> 00:00:41.953
Someone has to read each
one and pick the right owner.

11
00:00:41.953 --> 00:00:45.560
That’s the job we’re about
to hand to an automation.

12
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.454
First, let’s see where
automations come from.

13
00:00:52.454 --> 00:00:56.937
We open Automations in the
sidebar… and switch to the All tab.

14
00:00:56.937 --> 00:01:02.939
This is the catalog: ready-made bundles,
like syncing a mailbox or resolving GitHub

15
00:01:02.939 --> 00:01:03.639
issues.

16
00:01:03.639 --> 00:01:06.337
One install, and they set themselves up.

17
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.364
Before installing
anything, you can look inside.

18
00:01:12.364 --> 00:01:16.709
We click a bundle… and a panel
opens: these are the workflows,

19
00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:18.882
agents, and views it would add.

20
00:01:18.882 --> 00:01:20.074
Nothing runs yet.

21
00:01:20.074 --> 00:01:24.840
We close it again — because what
we need today is already installed.

22
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.275
Here, under Installed: task triage.

23
00:01:30.275 --> 00:01:35.150
It came with this workspace and has
been running quietly in the background.

24
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:40.480
Let’s open it and read what it actually
does — always worth doing before you trust

25
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.390
an automation.

26
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.741
This is the workflow
behind the automation.

27
00:01:49.741 --> 00:01:52.663
Take a second to read it, top to bottom.

28
00:01:52.663 --> 00:01:54.562
A trigger: every new task.

29
00:01:54.562 --> 00:01:57.776
Then three steps — an
agent scores the task,

30
00:01:57.776 --> 00:02:01.794
the score decides the route,
and a report gets written.

31
00:02:01.794 --> 00:02:03.182
That’s all it does.

32
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.756
Now let’s click the score
step and look at it up close.

33
00:02:09.756 --> 00:02:12.966
Here’s what the agent
must return: a candidate,

34
00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:16.449
a confidence number, and a
reason — a fixed format,

35
00:02:16.449 --> 00:02:17.542
called a schema.

36
00:02:17.542 --> 00:02:22.323
If the model returns anything else,
the run stops instead of guessing.

37
00:02:22.323 --> 00:02:26.080
Remember this schema — we’ll
meet it again in a minute.

38
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:30.560
Next tab over: Triggers.

39
00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.620
This is when the automation
wakes up — for every new task,

40
00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:38.540
and whenever someone moves a
task from Backlog to To do.

41
00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:43.440
Tasks that already have an owner
are skipped: nothing to triage there.

42
00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:49.093
There’s also a safe way
to try it: Test workflow.

43
00:02:49.093 --> 00:02:51.796
It shows exactly what input a run expects,

44
00:02:51.796 --> 00:02:55.142
and Execute would write a
real run into the journal.

45
00:02:55.142 --> 00:02:59.646
We’ll do something better, though —
trigger it the way your team will,

46
00:02:59.646 --> 00:03:00.740
with a real task.

47
00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:07.861
Back on the board.

48
00:03:07.861 --> 00:03:13.879
We create a task — ‘Prepare the
launch-day social posts’ — and now… we don’t touch

49
00:03:13.879 --> 00:03:14.579
anything.

50
00:03:14.579 --> 00:03:15.680
Watch the card.

51
00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.377
A few seconds pass… and there:
an assignee appeared, on its own.

52
00:03:20.377 --> 00:03:25.180
The automation read the task,
scored it, and picked the Assistant.

53
00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.503
Let’s verify that.

54
00:03:35.503 --> 00:03:40.653
We go back to Automations… open
task triage… and its Executions tab.

55
00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:43.834
There it is — the newest run, seconds old.

56
00:03:43.834 --> 00:03:47.166
We open it, and now
you can read everything:

57
00:03:47.166 --> 00:03:50.801
what the trigger saw,
the score with its reason,

58
00:03:50.801 --> 00:03:52.620
and what each step cost.

59
00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:02.814
Now, the run you’ll learn
the most from — this red one.

60
00:04:02.814 --> 00:04:05.754
Let’s open it and read
the journal together.

61
00:04:05.754 --> 00:04:09.696
The trigger fired, the score
step ran… and here it stopped:

62
00:04:09.696 --> 00:04:13.237
the agent’s answer was
missing the confidence number,

63
00:04:13.237 --> 00:04:14.974
so the schema rejected it.

64
00:04:14.974 --> 00:04:19.650
The run failed — on purpose —
instead of assigning a task on bad data.

65
00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.150
And fixing it has a clear path.

66
00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:31.069
The journal named the step; the
step holds the prompt and the schema;

67
00:04:31.069 --> 00:04:34.491
and under Configuration
you’ll find the retries.

68
00:04:34.491 --> 00:04:38.340
Journal, step, fix, run
again — that’s the whole loop.

69
00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:47.465
One question is left: what happens when
an automation wants to reach the outside

70
00:04:47.465 --> 00:04:48.165
world?

71
00:04:48.165 --> 00:04:49.696
Let’s find out in chat.

72
00:04:49.696 --> 00:04:54.289
We ask the Assistant to draft a
reply to a customer — and to send it.

73
00:04:54.289 --> 00:04:57.284
The draft appears… and
then everything stops.

74
00:04:57.284 --> 00:05:01.710
Sending an email leaves the
workspace, so this step waits for a person.

75
00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:16.457
Take your time and read the draft.

76
00:05:16.457 --> 00:05:17.157
It’s good.

77
00:05:17.157 --> 00:05:20.223
We add a short note…
and submit the response.

78
00:05:20.223 --> 00:05:22.880
Only now does the mail actually go out.

79
00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:26.541
The agent wrote it — but
sending it was your decision.

80
00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:37.529
One last stop: the audit log, in Settings.

81
00:05:37.529 --> 00:05:42.013
Here’s the approval we just made
— who decided, what, and when.

82
00:05:42.013 --> 00:05:46.782
If anyone ever asks who sent that
mail, you don’t have to remember.

83
00:05:46.782 --> 00:05:47.850
This page does.

84
00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:52.711
And that’s the episode.

85
00:05:52.711 --> 00:05:56.876
You read a workflow before
trusting it, triggered a real run,

86
00:05:56.876 --> 00:06:02.407
followed its journal, traced a failure to
its step — and kept the final say on an

87
00:06:02.407 --> 00:06:03.431
outgoing email.

88
00:06:03.431 --> 00:06:07.870
The docs on automations and
approvals take each of these further.

89
00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:14.978
Next time: projects — where your
team and its agents share one board.

90
00:06:14.978 --> 00:06:16.680
See you in episode six.

